<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Radical Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Radical Party was a Serbian political party founded in 1881 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> and others, with a strong base among the peasantry. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-coup-serbia">May Coup</a> of 1903 it dominated the parliamentary governments of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and it remained a leading party in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> until parties were banned under the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of 1929.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of a circle of young Serbian followers of Svetozar Marković and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a> in Zurich, whose programme called for a new constitution, freedom of the press, judicial independence, reform of education and stronger local self-government. Its first main assembly met in Kragujevac in July 1882, adopted a programme inspired by French Radicalism and elected Pašić president of the central committee. Over its history the party moved from socialism and radicalism towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> in the early 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Radicals published their own daily newspaper, Samouprava (Self-Government), which criticised the monarchy and demanded democracy, public liberties and reform of the bureaucracy. Its leaders, among them Pera Todorovic, Sima Lozanić, Lazar Paču, Raša Milošević and Stojan Protić, were the first to mobilise the Serbian peasantry and the provincial middle classes, including teachers, peasant leaders and priests, and the party also drew intellectuals and diplomats such as Milovan Milovanović and Milenko Vesnić. It supported uniting all Serb-inhabited lands under the slogan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> to the Balkan nations, and in foreign policy it was anti-Austrian, mostly Russophile and Francophile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In September 1883 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-i-of-serbia">Milan Obrenović</a> ordered the army to confiscate the peasants' arms, and the Timok Rebellion broke out in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. The king blamed an article in Samouprava; the rising was put down in ten days, most of the party's leaders were captured and many sentenced to death, while Pašić escaped to the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. An amnesty followed for Radicals who entered the government in 1887, and the party was instrumental in the parliamentary Constitution of 1888, after which it held almost 80 percent of the vote.</p><p>A dissident faction formed in 1901 and became the Independent Radical Party in 1905. After the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadordevic-dynasty">Karađorđević dynasty</a> in 1903, Radical governments led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> through its Golden Age and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and in 1917 Pašić signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a>. In the new kingdom the party held the premiership for eight of the years from 1918 to 1928, helped by electoral rules that favoured Serbia. After Pašić died in 1926, Aca Stanojević led the party and later called for an end to the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Radical Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…6 March 1882 until his abdication on 6 March 1889, after the lost war and the total electoral victory of the <b>People's Radical Party</b>, which under Sava Grujić and Nikola Pašić had come to power in 1888 with a new constitution based on the liberal Constitution of Belgium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Serbian parties, above all Nikola Pašić’s <b>People’s Radical Party</b>, dominated governments, the army and the civil service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
